In article <14588@cs.utexas.edu> varvel@cs.utexas.edu (Donald A. Varvel) writes:
>Kicking the chest will never (in my experience) destroy
>the chest, but can cost hit points and leave your leg (or
>hind limb, depending) in no shape to kick.  This also
>temporarily reduces your strength.  In addition, there's
>still the possibility of a trapped chest.

I just can't let this error go untouched.  Hurting your leg lowers your
dexterity by 1 point for a little while, and your carrying capacity goes
down the drain for that same period.

My standard procedure for chest opening (with a strong character) is to
attempt to untrap it (about 10 times or so), just to check for traps.  If
the chest is trapped, I don't touch it unless I have shock resistance,
fire resistance and poison resistance.  In those cases, I'll actually try
to disarm.

If the chest is untrapped (either originally or as a result of disarming or
setting off the trap), I attempt to loot it.  If it is locked, I kick until
the lock breaks.  (This rarely requires more than two or three good whacks,
if I have warrior-type strength.  Wizards take longer, unless I've met a few
giants....)

